# Line endings.
#
# This repo is developed on Windows, where core.autocrlf=true is the norm and
# every text file is checked out with CRLF. That is harmless for .py/.ts/.md —
# and fatal for a shell script: bash reads the trailing \r as part of the
# command and fails with the famously unhelpful "\r: command not found", or
# "#!/usr/bin/env bash\r: no such file or directory". So shell scripts are
# pinned to LF no matter what the cloner's autocrlf says.
*.sh text eol=lf

# The mirror image: PowerShell and the .cmd wrapper are Windows-side tools and
# stay CRLF, which is what a Windows editor and cmd.exe expect.
*.ps1 text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf

# The frozen deployment's approved inputs are CONTENT-ADDRESSED: frozen/
# approved-install.json pins each one's SHA-256, and the manifest's own digest
# names the container image tags. "let git decide locally" is exactly wrong
# for those files — the same commit would hash differently on Windows and
# Linux, so a manifest approved on one platform could never verify on the
# other, and the image tag would move without a single byte being authored.
#
# It is not only a hashing concern. frozen/sandbox-apt.txt is fed to apt
# through xargs, so a CRLF checkout asks for `sudo=1.9.13p3-1+deb12u4\r` and
# the sandbox image FAILS TO BUILD on the very platform this repo is
# developed on ("E: Version '...' for 'sudo' was not found").
frozen/** text eol=lf
frontend/package-lock.json text eol=lf
backend/orgtree/bridgeauth.py text eol=lf
backend/orgtree/frozen_gateway.py text eol=lf

# Everything else: normalise to LF in the repository, let git decide locally.
* text=auto
